8. Methodology
1. Import Pandas and Matplotlib and enable inline plotting.
2. Load the CSV file into a Pandas DataFrame.
3. Inspect the dataset with info() and describe().
4. Convert release_date to datetime, coercing invalid dates to missing values.
5. Create year and month columns from release_date.
6. Check missing values using isna().sum().
7. Create a cleaned DataFrame by removing rows missing release_date, popularity, or vote_average.
8. Perform exploratory analysis using histograms, bar charts, a scatter plot, and a correlation matrix/heatmap.
9. Identify the top 10 popular movies.
10. For top-rated analysis, keep movies with at least 50 votes and then sort by vote_average.
9. Analysis and Visualizations
•	Distribution of Movie Popularity: shows how popularity values are distributed.
•	Distribution of Movie Ratings: shows the spread of average movie ratings.
•	Top 10 Languages by Number of Movies: shows the most common original languages.
•	Average Rating by Original Language: compares average ratings for languages represented by at least 20 movies.
•	Average Popularity by Release Month: compares mean popularity across months.
•	Number of Movies Released by Month: shows the release volume for each month.
•	Popularity vs Rating: scatter plot used to visually examine the relationship between rating and popularity.
•	Correlation Matrix and Heatmap: measures linear relationships among numeric variables.
•	Top Popular Movies: lists the 10 movies with the highest popularity values.
•	Top Rated Movies: lists the 10 highest-rated movies among movies with at least 50 votes.
10. Key Findings
•	Most movie ratings are concentrated between approximately 5 and 7 according to the project README.
•	The README reports that popularity is highest for movies released in July, September, and October.
•	English is reported as the most common original language.
•	The project reports a weak relationship between popularity and ratings.
•	A high rating does not necessarily correspond to high popularity in this dataset.
•	The notebook uses a minimum of 50 votes when selecting top-rated movies to reduce the effect of movies with very few votes.
11. Conclusion
The project demonstrates how Python-based exploratory data analysis can be used to understand movie datasets. The analysis indicates that popularity cannot be explained by ratings alone. Release timing, language representation, audience interest, and other factors can be associated with popularity. The project provides a practical workflow covering data loading, cleaning, transformation, visualization, correlation analysis, and ranking.
